<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Newton is a 50-year-old man treated for major depression and comorbid insomnia, one week after starting sertraline 50 mg, who came in the same day with a cold. Two days ago he developed a scratchy throat and runny nose, then a dry cough and aches; his temperature at home was 37.9 C last night. Several of his students have had colds. He is eating and drinking normally. He has no shortness of breath, chest pain, ear pain, facial pain, or rash. He asked whether he could take the night-time cold medicine sold at the college shop; he has not yet taken it. On sertraline he has had mild nausea for three days, now settled, and no diarrhea, headache, restlessness, or bleeding. He sleeps about four and a half hours with the CBT-I rules. He has had no thoughts of death, of harming himself, or of harming anyone. The laboratory remains closed. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days; sore throat and runny nose first, then cough</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Low-grade fever, aches, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Pertinent negatives</td><td>No breathlessness, chest pain, ear or sinus pain, or rash</td></tr><tr><td>Exposure</td><td>Colds among his students</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Sertraline 50 mg for one week; mild nausea that has settled; no cold remedies yet</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

