    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>A year of poor sleep and poor appetite with 4 kg weight loss, two weeks of near-total sleeplessness, low spirits, and suspicious thoughts that colleagues and friends were plotting against him, now partly doubted; under evaluation for depression, a toxic cause, and other medical causes</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R45.89</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern echoing the poor sleep, poor appetite, distress, and retracted suspicions documented in his letters of September 1693; superseded by the diagnoses at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Occupational exposure to elemental and inorganic mercury, with lesser exposure to lead, antimony, and arsenic compounds, from heating metals in an unventilated private laboratory</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z57.5</td><td>S — Synthetic modern exposure echoing his documented chemical and alchemical work with metals, including mercury; no level of historical exposure is asserted.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Major depressive disorder, single episode, severe, without psychotic features (PHQ-9 20 at intake): a year of poor sleep and appetite with weight loss, low mood, poor concentration, and suspicious ideas held with partial insight; medical causes excluded; the contribution of low-level mercury exposure is uncertain</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F32.2</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis. His sleeplessness, poor appetite, distress, and melancholy in 1693 are documented (D); calling them major depression is a modern reading, and mercury poisoning and other explanations proposed later are disputed (R).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Chronic insomnia comorbid with depression: sleep onset and maintenance difficulty for a year, with two weeks of near-total sleeplessness before intake</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G47.00</td><td>S — Synthetic diagnosis echoing his documented sleeplessness.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Raised urine mercury from occupational exposure (24-hour urine 46 ug/L, 41 ug/g creatinine; blood 9.4 ug/L) without tremor, gingivitis, erethism, neuropathy, or kidney injury; below the levels linked to overt toxicity</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R82.6</td><td>S — Synthetic result echoing his documented work with mercury; it does not assert that he was poisoned (see CLM-0011, R, disputed).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection (common cold) with a low-grade fever, sore throat, and cough, a week after starting sertraline; resolved</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent illness at an unscheduled visit; it echoes, without asserting, his documented mention of a distemper that was epidemical that summer (CLM-0008).</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

